how it works:
.feature file
   ↓
Cucumber
   ↓
Mapping Given / When / Then
   ↓
Step Definition (.ts)
   ↓
Playwright
   ↓
Browser
   ↓
Web Application


create first test case:
step 1: create a feature file
step 2: create a cucumber.json file for configuration under project folder 
step 3: edit package.json to convert default language JS to TS
step 4: run in VScode terminal $ npm test         //it looks at feature and step files
step 5: create a step file

note: 
get into cucumber extension installation page again, it shows up paths of feature files which is where we have to keep feature files.
OR
settings -> settings -> search cucumber -> select Extensions: cucumber -> Edit in settings.json -> copy Related Path of feature folder and step folder -> add to settings

example of cucumber.json:
{
    "default": {
        //make sure that one step starts after the previous one finishes (async function + await)
        //this setting is only for auto-generated snippets instead of existing steps
        "formatOptions": {
            "snippetInterface": "async-await"   
        },
        "paths": [
            "src/test/features/"
        ],
        "publishQuiet": true,
        "dryRun": true
        "require": [
            "src/test/steps/*.ts"
        ],
        "requireModule": [
            "ts-node/register"      //to solve the ts-import statement error
        ]
    }

an example of "async-await":
if feature file is as below:
Feature: Login
Scenario: Successful login
  Given I am on the login page
  When I enter my username
  Then I should see the homepage

and there's no step Definition of 'Given I am on the login page', when run $ npm test, cucumber would generate:
Given('I am on the login page', async function () {
  // Write code here
});

and we can add playwright into it:
Given('I am on the login page', async function () {
  await this.page.goto('/login');
});

which is this workflow:
Cucumber
   ↓
Generate async Step Definition
   ↓
we write Playwright
   ↓
await page.goto()
   ↓
Browser

known issue of compatibility between ts-node and TypeScript 7:
running the application or test framework (say, $ npm test) can fail with an error as below:
TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'fileExists')



